      <article class="ar-body">
        <p>The useful distinction is not which company ranks first. It is which <em>type</em> of supplier matches your volume, your fit, and your stage. This guide maps ten supplier types, gives you the evaluation framework to score any candidate against them, and discloses where our own facility sits</p>
        <hr>
        
        <h2 id="why-china">Why China Dominates Oversized T-Shirt Manufacturing</h2>
        <p>China's knitwear advantage is structural. China has consistently ranked among the world's largest apparel exporters by value in WTO trade statistics, and oversized tees sit inside the strongest part of that base: cluster density. Within a few hours' drive in Guangdong, Zhejiang, or Jiangsu, you can buy combed cotton yarn, commission a knit-and-dye mill, order matching ribbing, book a wash house for an enzyme or acid wash, and finish at a factory that cuts and sews drop-shoulder panels</p>
        <p>Oversized tees stress that chain in specific places. A standard crew neck runs 160–180 GSM. A credible oversized body lands at 220–280 GSM, and heavyweight streetwear builds push 280–320 GSM. Heavier fabric changes everything downstream: shrinkage climbs, collar ribbing needs more structure so it does not stretch under its own weight, and drop-shoulder seams carry more load at the armhole. A factory that only runs light jersey will quote confidently, then deliver a body that twists after one wash</p>
        <p>Three practical reasons buyers keep coming back:</p>
        <ul class="ar-list">
        <li><strong>Cost banding at volume.</strong> FOB pricing for a 240 GSM cotton oversized tee commonly falls in the $3.8–7.5/piece range at 3,000 pieces, depending on fabric origin, wash treatment, and print method. Treat that as a planning band, never a quote</li>
        <li><strong>MOQ flexibility.</strong> Chinese factories cover the full spectrum, from 100–300 pieces per color for small brands to 5,000+ for mass retail</li>
        <li><strong>Category depth.</strong> Print, embroidery, garment dye, and specialty washes sit in-house or next door, which compresses the sampling loop</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The catch: that depth is unevenly distributed. A factory that nails 180 GSM basics may be mediocre at 300 GSM boxy fits. Hence the framework matters more than the list</p>
        
        <hr>
        
        <h2 id="method">How We Evaluated These Oversized T-Shirt Suppliers</h2>
        <p>No ranking survives contact with a real brief unless the scoring is visible. We weighted six dimensions and deliberately put category specialization above scale, because a 2,000-worker generalist loses to a 200-worker oversized specialist on exactly the details that decide whether your fit sells</p>
        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table">
        <thead>
        <tr><th>Evaluation Dimension</th><th>Weight</th><th>What We Look For</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr><td>Oversized category specialization</td><td>25%</td><td>Evidence of drop-shoulder, boxy, and heavyweight (220–320 GSM) production; pattern blocks already on file</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MOQ flexibility</td><td>20%</td><td>Per-color minimums from 100–300 pieces (small brand tier) up to 1,000+ (mass tier); willingness to quote mixed-color orders</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sampling capability</td><td>20%</td><td>In-house or tightly controlled sample room; 5–15 working day sample turnaround as a common industry range</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Quality control system</td><td>15%</td><td>Documented inline and final inspection; shrinkage and colorfastness testing before shipment</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Compliance and certifications</td><td>10%</td><td>Verifiable audit documentation; buyers should request certificate numbers directly rather than rely on website badges</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Communication responsiveness</td><td>10%</td><td>English-language account management; quote turnaround within 24–72 hours as a working benchmark</td></tr>
        </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>This is a fit-based framework, not an absolute quality ladder: a supplier built for small-batch flexibility is not better than one built for container-scale production, only better for a specific buyer. Pricing, MOQ, and lead times shift with fabric, quantity, and season, so confirm every figure here against a live quote</p>
        <p>To see how we apply these standards internally, review <a href="/our-services/#group-05">our factory audit and quality control standards</a></p>
        
        <hr>
        
        <h2 id="supplier-types">The 10 Oversized T-Shirt Supplier Types in China (2026)</h2>
        <p>This list ranks supplier <em>types</em>, not individual companies; only YAUUM is named, because it is our own facility. Read the type labels before the numbering. They carry more information than the order does</p>
        <h3>1. Full-Category Multi-Product Manufacturers</h3>
        <p>Factories running tees, hoodies, jackets, and bottoms on one floor, with oversized blocks already developed rather than improvised per order. Best fit for brands building a coordinated capsule who want one accountable partner from sample to shipment. Watch-out: capacity windows tighten from roughly August through November, so confirm slots before locking a launch date</p>
        <h3>2. Guangdong Knit Cluster Factories (Dongguan / Guangzhou)</h3>
        <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Dense cluster of mid-to-large knitwear factories strong on 180–260 GSM jersey, reactive and pigment dyeing, and screen printing at volume</p>
        <p><strong>Best fit:</strong> Buyers running 1,000–5,000 pieces per style who need competitive FOB pricing and fast print turnaround</p>
        <p><strong>Watch-out:</strong> Quality swings sharply between factories inside the same industrial zone. Cluster proximity is not a quality signal; audit each facility individually</p>
        <h3>3. Zhejiang Export-Oriented Knitwear Mills (Ningbo / Shaoxing)</h3>
        <p>Export programs for European and North American retail have shaped these mills around consistency: 160–220 GSM basics, dependable color matching across repeat orders, and compliance paperwork that survives retail audits. They suit replenishment buyers who value color continuity over trend speed. The limitation is weight — heavyweight 280 GSM+ streetwear is rarely their default, so verify fabric supply before paying for sampling</p>
        <h3>4. Jiangsu Mid-Weight Specialists</h3>
        <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Factories concentrated on 200–260 GSM cotton and cotton-blend tees with tight shrinkage and measurement tolerances</p>
        <p><strong>Best fit:</strong> DTC brands whose return rates react to fit drift</p>
        <p><strong>Watch-out:</strong> Specialty wash capability is thin. Acid wash and heavy garment dye often get outsourced, adding a week or more to lead time</p>
        <h3>5. Fujian Sportswear-Adjacent Knit Factories</h3>
        <p>Rooted in performance and athleisure production, these factories bring cotton-poly blends, moisture-management finishes, and clean flatlock and coverstitch construction to oversized silhouettes. They are the right call when the brief is a hybrid casual-sport fit. They are the wrong call when the brief is 100% cotton heavyweight streetwear, so pin down fabric sourcing in the first call</p>
        <h3>6. Shandong Large-Volume Basic Tee Producers</h3>
        <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> High-capacity producers built for container-scale basic knitwear at 160–200 GSM</p>
        <p><strong>Best fit:</strong> Mass retail and promotional buyers ordering 10,000+ pieces per style</p>
        <p><strong>Watch-out:</strong> High minimums and little appetite for small batches or experimental washes. The wrong partner for a first drop</p>
        <h3>7. Small-Batch / Low-MOQ Oversized Specialists</h3>
        <p>Smaller factories and studios organized around 100–300 piece per-color runs, rapid sample iteration, and mixed-color orders. They lower financial risk per style, which is what a first collection actually needs. Expect unit costs well above volume tiers, and expect capacity to tighten in peak season; book sample and production slots early</p>
        <h3>8. Sustainable and Certified Production Facilities</h3>
        <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Manufacturers organized around organic and recycled cotton programs with documented traceability and lower-impact dye processes</p>
        <p><strong>Best fit:</strong> Brands whose marketing rests on verifiable material claims</p>
        <p><strong>Watch-out:</strong> Higher unit costs and longer lead times. Check that the certificate covers the specific facility and product line, not just a parent group</p>
        <h3>9. Full-Package OEM/ODM Developers</h3>
        <p>These suppliers take a mood board or a reference garment and return a production-ready oversized block with grading and construction specs included, which removes any dependency on external pattern services. They fit brands without an in-house technical designer. Settle development fees and IP ownership in writing before you share a single design file</p>
        <h3>10. Trading Companies with Vetted Factory Networks</h3>
        <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Sourcing intermediaries coordinating several factories rather than manufacturing in-house, useful for placing tees, hoodies, and accessories in one order cycle</p>
        <p><strong>Best fit:</strong> Buyers assembling a wide, shallow range who want one point of contact</p>
        <p><strong>Watch-out:</strong> Quality accountability sits one step removed from the floor. Insist on factory disclosure and third-party inspection</p>
        <hr>
        <h3>YAUUM (Our Own Facility — Disclosed)</h3>
        <p>We fall into type 1 above, and we are naming ourselves rather than quietly ranking ourselves first. YAUUM is a B2B men's apparel manufacturer covering hoodies, t-shirts, jackets, pants, sportswear, and streetwear, with oversized tees produced across the 220–300 GSM band on drop-shoulder and boxy blocks</p>
        <p>Because the same floor runs tees, hoodies, and streetwear, a brand can build a coordinated capsule without splitting production across vendors, and custom labels, neck tags, and packaging move through the order itself instead of arriving as third-party add-ons. Sample and bulk stay in the same facility. Judge that claim the way you would judge any supplier on this page: against the six criteria above, with a sample in hand</p>
