        <hr>
        
        <h2 id="specs">Oversized T-Shirt Manufacturing: What Actually Matters</h2>
        
        <p>Nearly every sourcing failure on oversized tees traces back to four technical decisions made before the cutting table</p>
        <p><strong>Fabric weight and construction.</strong> GSM drives perceived quality, but weight alone tells you little. A 260 GSM single jersey and a 260 GSM French terry behave nothing alike on a drop-shoulder body. For oversized silhouettes, 220–280 GSM single jersey is the working range across most streetwear programs, with 280–320 GSM reserved for builds where structure is the whole point. Ask for the fabric spec sheet, not the marketing description</p>
        <p><strong>Drop-shoulder pattern engineering.</strong> Oversized is a pattern decision, never a sizing decision. A true drop-shoulder block moves the shoulder seam down the arm, which rewrites armhole depth, sleeve pitch, and drape. Factories that simply grade up a standard block hand you a tee that looks large instead of intentionally oversized. Ask to see the block and a photo from a previous drop-shoulder run</p>
        <p><strong>Wash and shrinkage control.</strong> Every garment-dyed or washed tee shrinks. The only question is whether the factory controls it or your customer discovers it. Pre-shrunk and compacted fabrics cut residual shrinkage, and a factory running its own wash trials will hand you tested shrinkage percentages per colorway</p>
        <p><strong>Sampling-to-bulk consistency.</strong> A perfect sample means nothing when bulk fabric arrives from a different mill lot. The control point is lot reservation: the factory should reserve and record the fabric lot behind the approved sample, then draw bulk from that lot wherever volume allows</p>
        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table">
        <thead>
        <tr><th>Fabric Type</th><th>Typical GSM Range</th><th>Best For</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr><td>100% combed cotton single jersey</td><td>180–240</td><td>Everyday oversized tees</td><td>Moderate shrinkage; needs pre-shrinking</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heavyweight cotton jersey</td><td>240–320</td><td>Streetwear, boxy fits</td><td>Higher cost; longer wash cycles</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cotton-poly blend</td><td>180–240</td><td>Athleisure, lower-shrink programs</td><td>Less natural hand feel</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Organic / recycled cotton</td><td>180–260</td><td>Sustainability-led brands</td><td>Higher cost; longer lead time</td></tr>
        <tr><td>French terry</td><td>240–320</td><td>Oversized tees with structure, matching hoodies</td><td>Heavier freight cost</td></tr>
        </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>For a closer look at how these choices play out on the floor, see our <a href="/t-shirt-manufacturer/#tshirt-process">custom oversized t-shirt manufacturing process</a></p>
        
        <hr>
        
        <h2 id="choose">How to Choose the Right Supplier for Your Brand</h2>
        
        <p>The right supplier depends on your stage, not on a universal ranking</p>
        <p><strong>Startup or first collection (100–300 pieces per color).</strong> Prioritize low-MOQ specialists and fast sample loops. You are buying information as much as product: how the fit sells, which colorways move, where returns land. Unit cost is secondary at this volume. Explore <a href="/production/#important-note">low MOQ oversized t-shirt options</a></p>
        <p><strong>Growth stage (500–2,000 pieces per style).</strong> Repeat-order consistency becomes the constraint. Look for reserved fabric lots, documented shrinkage testing, and a track record on replenishment programs. Switching costs climb quickly here, so invest in the relationship rather than chasing quotes</p>
        <p><strong>Mature brand (2,000+ pieces per style).</strong> Capacity reliability, compliance documentation, and multi-category capability for vendor consolidation take priority. At this scale, a 5% unit cost difference matters far less than a two-week delay on a seasonal drop</p>
        <p>Before you send a single inquiry, prepare four things:</p>
        <ol class="ar-list">
        <li><strong>A reference garment or clear fit spec.</strong> Photos plus measurements (chest, body length, shoulder drop, sleeve opening) beat any written description</li>
        <li><strong>Fabric requirements.</strong> Target GSM, composition, and whether you need a specific wash or finish</li>
        <li><strong>Quantity estimate by colorway.</strong> This sets your MOQ tier and your pricing band</li>
        <li><strong>Target price and timeline.</strong> Stating both upfront filters out mismatched suppliers in a single round</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Then run the process in order: inquiry and capability screening, sample development, sample approval against written specs, bulk production, inline and final inspection, shipment. Never skip the written spec at sample approval. Verbal approvals are where disputes begin</p>
        
        <hr>
        
        <h2 id="mistakes">Common Mistakes When Sourcing Oversized T-Shirts from China</h2>
        <p><strong>Skipping the sample, or rushing it.</strong> A sample is the cheapest place on earth to find a problem. Approve one without measuring it against your spec and a 2 cm shoulder discrepancy will be replicated across all 3,000 units</p>
        <p>Verification gets skipped more often than it should. Trading companies presenting as factories remains common, so ask for the production address, request a video walkthrough, and commission third-party inspection on your first bulk order</p>
        <p><strong>Ignoring shrinkage and colorfastness testing.</strong> Request test results before shipment, not after the complaints land. A <a href="/t-shirt-manufacturer/#tshirt-quality">t-shirt quality control checklist</a> standardizes what you ask for</p>
        <p>Labels and packaging are the last items buyers think about and the first to delay a shipment. Custom neck tags, hangtags, and polybag specs carry their own lead times, so lock them at sample stage rather than after bulk approval</p>
        
        <hr>
        
        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <details class="ar-faq-item" open><summary><h3>What is the typical MOQ for custom oversized t-shirts from China?</h3></summary><p>MOQ varies by factory tier. Small-batch specialists commonly open at 100–300 pieces per color, mid-size factories at 500–1,000 pieces per color, and large-volume producers at 2,000+ pieces per style. Fabric choice affects this: specialty or custom-dyed fabric usually raises the minimum. Confirm the per-color minimum, not just the per-order minimum</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How long does sampling take for oversized t-shirts?</h3></summary><p>Sampling typically runs 7–15 working days for a standard cotton oversized tee, and 15–25 working days when custom dyeing, washing, or specialty printing is involved. Add shipping time for the sample itself. Peak season can extend these windows, so build buffer into your launch timeline</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Can I get custom labels and packaging for oversized tees?</h3></summary><p>Yes. Custom woven or printed neck labels, hangtags, size tags, and polybag printing are standard services at most export-oriented factories. Provide artwork and specs at sample stage so labels are approved alongside the garment. Some factories handle this in-house; others coordinate with local suppliers, which adds a few days</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How do I verify a Chinese oversized t-shirt manufacturer?</h3></summary><p>Request the production address, then confirm it matches the business license. Ask for a live video walkthrough of the production floor, request references from comparable buyers, and check whether the factory can show recent inspection reports. For a first order, commission a third-party inspection before shipment. A factory that resists these steps is telling you something</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What certifications should I look for in a t-shirt manufacturer?</h3></summary><p>The relevant certifications depend on your market and claims. Common programs cover social compliance, quality management, and organic or recycled material traceability. Always request the certificate number and verify it directly with the issuing body, and confirm the certificate covers the specific facility and product line you are ordering from</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How much does it cost to manufacture oversized t-shirts in China?</h3></summary><p>For a 240 GSM cotton oversized tee at 3,000 pieces, FOB pricing commonly falls in the $3.8–7.5/piece range, with heavier fabrics, specialty washes, and complex printing pushing above that band. Small-batch orders under 300 pieces carry higher unit costs. These are planning ranges only; request a live quote against your spec</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Can small brands order low quantities of oversized t-shirts?</h3></summary><p>Yes. A meaningful segment of Chinese factories is built specifically around 100–300 piece per-color runs for emerging brands. Expect higher unit pricing and tighter capacity during peak season. Booking sample slots early matters more at low volume than at high volume</p></details>
        
